    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Although there have been many studies on unsteady behavior of veils with a partially-penetrating wellbore, there has been no study of the combined effects of wellbore damage and storage on the behavior of partially-penetrating veils. The purpose of this study is to fill this existing gap in knowledge by providing a thorough study of the effects of wellbore damage and storage on behavior of a finite radius partially-penetrating well. Consideration was also given to improving interpretation techniques for short-time buildup and drawdown analyses. Specifically, this study was made on a bounded, anisotropic, cylindrical reservoir with a partially-penetrating, infinite conductivity cylindrical inner boundary. This inner boundary also contained wellbore storage and a flux dependent infinitesimal (zero storage capacity) skin effect. A careful analysis of the pressure versus time graph for a partially-penetrating well indicated the existence of two semi-log straight line periods. Either straight line can be interpreted to yield the horizontal permeability and the skin effect. However, the determination, of the true skin effect from the second semi-log straight line requires knowledge of pseudo skin effect. Vertical permeability can be evaluated from correlations relating either the ending time of the first straight line, the intersection time of the two straight lines, or the \"beginning time to the second straight line to the effective vertical permeability. All three methods may be destroyed if wellbore storage is significant.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
